WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.020
After a hard sprint, your legs may burn.

2
00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:09.720
Bread dough puffs up with bubbles.

3
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:15.520
In summer, parts of the deep Chesapeake Bay hold very little oxygen.

4
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.800
All three are about one question.

5
00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:25.860
How does the energy in food become energy a cell can use, with or without oxygen?

6
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:33.040
Cells break down glucose, a sugar, in a process called cellular respiration.

7
00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.500
The first steps happen in the cytoplasm.

8
00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:43.380
Most of the ATP is made in the mitochondria, which are organelles in plant, animal

9
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:49.560
and fungal cells. With oxygen, glucose becomes carbon dioxide and water.

10
00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:55.040
Much of the released energy is captured in a small molecule called ATP.

11
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.580
ATP is a molecule with three phosphate groups in a row.

12
00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:05.480
A phosphate group is a cluster of atoms that can be added or removed.

13
00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.180
When ATP reacts with water, the end phosphate group comes off.

14
00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.600
What is left is ADP, with two phosphate groups.

15
00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.860
The reaction releases energy that the cell uses for work.

16
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.520
ATP is not energy.

17
00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.020
It is a molecule that carries energy.

18
00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:30.330
Putting a phosphate group back onto ADP takes energy.

19
00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.650
Cellular respiration supplies that energy.

20
00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:38.050
So ATP and ADP go round in a cycle.

21
00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:42.170
Cell work turns ATP into ADP.

22
00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:46.350
Respiration turns ADP back into ATP.

23
00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:52.510
A cell keeps only a small supply of ATP, so it rebuilds it all the time.

24
00:01:54.783 --> 00:01:57.363
Aerobic means using oxygen.

25
00:01:58.363 --> 00:02:00.563
Anaerobic means without oxygen.

26
00:02:01.803 --> 00:02:05.703
Without oxygen, muscle cells and yeast use fermentation.

27
00:02:06.543 --> 00:02:09.383
It makes a small amount of ATP from glucose.

28
00:02:10.403 --> 00:02:13.143
The SOL calls this anaerobic respiration.

29
00:02:14.303 --> 00:02:16.843
In muscle, fermentation makes lactate.

30
00:02:17.443 --> 00:02:20.243
In yeast, it makes ethanol and carbon dioxide.

31
00:02:20.983 --> 00:02:24.483
The carbon dioxide forms the bubbles that make dough rise.

32
00:02:25.333 --> 00:02:30.533
With oxygen, one glucose gives about 30 to 32 ATP.

33
00:02:31.553 --> 00:02:33.313
Fermentation gives only two.

34
00:02:34.173 --> 00:02:40.453
But by one 2024 estimate, fermentation makes ATP faster for the same amount of

35
00:02:40.453 --> 00:02:42.893
enzyme, in yeast and mammal cells.

36
00:02:43.773 --> 00:02:49.633
In a sprint, muscles use ATP faster than aerobic respiration alone can rebuild it.

37
00:02:49.713 --> 00:02:52.033
So fermentation adds ATP.

38
00:02:52.033 --> 00:02:58.813
Food energy reaches cell work through ATP, rebuilt with oxygen or without it.
